   "start_page": 241,
   "summary": "This report section details Japan's China policy and opposition to third-power financial assistance, followed by negotiations with the Soviet Union over the Chinese Eastern Railway, border incidents, and Northern Pacific fisheries. It reviews the contentious trade negotiations with the Netherlands East Indies in Batavia, British textile quota restrictions, trade talks with Australia, Brazilian restrictions on Japanese immigration, and El Salvador's diplomatic recognition of Manchukuo. Domestically, it examines the political crisis caused by the Kuroda bribery scandal in the Finance Ministry, prospective successors to Premier Saito, Army and Navy leadership shifts, rural economic distress involving rice surpluses and fertilizers, and corporate developments in the automobile and oil sectors.",
   "title": "Report on Conditions in Japan During the Month of May, 1934",
